 7  17.96- 20.23  Chapter one. The Spice Problem.
 8  20.83- 22.27  In fourteen fifty-three,
 9  22.65- 25.12  the Ottoman Turks captured Constantinople,
10  25.54- 28.65  and the old roads to the East fell into other hands.
11  28.95- 31.93  Indian pepper, silk and cotton still reached Europe,
12  32.40- 34.14  but only through Arab middlemen,
13  34.46- 36.17  and every one of them took a cut.
14  36.47- 39.45  So little Portugal, on the edge of the Atlantic,
15  39.63- 40.70  made a bold bet:
16  41.10- 43.12  if you can't go through, go around.
17  43.42- 45.04  Prince Henry, the Navigator,
18  45.38- 47.93  pushed ship after ship down the coast of Africa,
19  48.37- 53.16  and in fourteen eighty-eight, Bartholomew Dias rounded the Cape of Good Hope.
20  53.46- 56.24  The door to the Indian Ocean was open.
21  56.94- 59.21  Chapter two. Hello, Kalikut.
22  59.81- 63.20  Ten years later, Vasco da Gama walked through it.
23  63.50- 66.67  In East Africa, he picked up a Gujarati pilot
24  66.90- 68.52  who knew the monsoon winds,
25  68.93- 71.94  and in May fourteen ninety-eight, he reached Kalikut.
26  72.24- 74.72  Kalikut's Hindu king, the Zam-oh-rin,
27  75.06- 76.91  grew rich by welcoming traders,
28  77.26- 78.95  so he welcomed this one too.
29  79.25- 81.88  The Arab merchants were far less happy.
30  82.17- 86.05  For centuries, Indians, Arabs, Africans and Chinese
31  86.05- 88.75  had shared this ocean by unwritten rules:
32  89.19- 91.83  everyone traded, but nobody owned the sea.
33  92.14- 94.49  The Portuguese wanted to own it.
